WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.933
A bank in Baku lends to two construction firms, both in the same

00:04.033 --> 00:08.281
sector. If one of them defaults, should the bank worry more about the

00:08.381 --> 00:12.566
other? And how does that change the risk of its loan book? Answering

00:12.666 --> 00:16.095
this uses every idea of Chapter five, one after another.

00:18.183 --> 00:21.009
So let's walk the chain once, from start to finish.

00:22.383 --> 00:25.947
Step one, the joint distribution. D one and D two

00:26.047 --> 00:29.612
are one if the firm defaults within the year. The

00:29.712 --> 00:34.025
table gives the probability of each pair: both survive with

00:34.125 --> 00:38.662
probability point nine zero, both default with point zero two.

00:38.750 --> 00:42.237
Step two, the marginals. Add along the row, and firm one

00:42.337 --> 00:46.721
defaults with probability six percent. By symmetry, firm two does too.

00:46.816 --> 00:51.305
Step three, conditioning. Suppose firm one has defaulted: keep only that

00:51.405 --> 00:54.237
row, and rescale it to add up to one. Now firm

00:54.337 --> 00:58.507
two defaults with probability one third. It jumped from six percent

00:58.607 --> 01:02.076
to thirty-three. So the two are clearly not independent.

01:02.166 --> 01:05.558
Step four puts a number on it. The covariance is the

01:05.658 --> 01:09.788
expected product minus the product of the means: point zero one

01:09.888 --> 01:13.011
six four, a correlation of about point two nine.

01:14.100 --> 01:17.759
Step five, a linear function. With forty-five percent lost

01:17.859 --> 01:21.129
on default, the bank's loss is thirty-six thousand D

01:21.229 --> 01:24.565
one plus fifty-four thousand D two. The expected loss

01:24.665 --> 01:27.871
needs only the marginals: fifty-four hundred manat.

01:27.966 --> 01:31.372
The spread does not. If the firms were independent, the

01:31.472 --> 01:35.706
standard deviation would be about fifteen thousand four hundred. The

01:35.806 --> 01:39.212
positive covariance adds a term, and pushes it to about

01:39.312 --> 01:43.866
seventeen thousand four hundred. Same-sector loans fail together, and the

01:43.966 --> 01:45.969
variance of a sum prices that in.

01:47.066 --> 01:52.209
The same chain prices a portfolio. Sixty percent in a bank-share fund,

01:52.309 --> 01:57.602
forty in a bond fund, with a slightly negative correlation. The variance

01:57.702 --> 02:02.919
of the mix uses the same formula: squared weights times variances, plus

02:03.019 --> 02:07.263
the covariance term. It comes out at eight point six four.

02:07.350 --> 02:11.592
Now assume normality, and bring in Chapter four. The chance of losing

02:11.692 --> 02:15.556
more than three percent in a month: twenty percent for the bank

02:15.656 --> 02:19.206
fund alone, but only eight and a half percent for the mix.

02:19.306 --> 02:23.863
Chapter five gave the variance; Chapter four turned it into a probability.

02:25.950 --> 02:29.982
So Chapter five is one chain. The joint distribution holds

02:30.082 --> 02:35.326
everything. Marginals add it up, conditionals slice it, covariance measures

02:35.426 --> 02:39.102
how the variables move together, and for any sum, the

02:39.202 --> 02:43.591
means just add, while the variance needs every covariance. That

02:43.691 --> 02:47.296
is exactly the toolkit that inference will build on.
